      \field{labeltitlesource}{title}
      \field{abstract}{This Recommendation defines applications, device types, operational considerations and transmission limits for optical amplifiers (OAs) used in optical systems. It classifies OAs as booster (BA), pre-amplifier (PA), line amplifier (LA), optically amplified transmitter (OAT) and receiver (OAR), and notes relevant system interfaces and parameter references (notably the ITU‑T G.690-series and G.662/G.661). BAs raise transmitter power but increase non-linear penalties; PAs improve receiver sensitivity and benefit from narrow-band filtering; LAs extend spans and require supervisory channels; OATs/OARs integrate amplifiers with transceivers. Single- and multi-channel, point-to-point and point-to-multipoint uses are covered; many multi-channel parameters remain under study. Environmental and optical safety requirements and APR procedures are referenced.Transmission-related impairments from increased launch power and long unregenerated spans are detailed: fibre non-linearities (SPM, IXPM/XPM, FWM/IFWM, MI, SBS, SRS), polarization effects (PMD, PDL, polarization hole burning), chromatic dispersion, and OA-specific issues (noise accumulation, self‑filtering, optical surge, SOA saturation-induced non-linearity). These impairments depend on fibre type, effective area, channel spacing, bit rate, modulation format and amplifier cascade.Mitigation strategies include fibre selection and dispersion management, appropriate channel spacing or uneven spacing, reduced launch power, increased effective area, narrow-band ASE filtering or self‑filtering, polarization scrambling and dynamic PMD compensation, ASE-filtering and per-channel equalization, gain-clamping or operating SOAs in linear regime, and pump control/APR to prevent optical surge. Appendix I gives example BA/PA parameter values; OAM items and alarms for OAs are specified.}
